hi I'm Nick Gillespie and I'm with Matt  Welch my co-author of the Declaration of  Independence how libertarian politics  can fix what's wrong with America today  is ask a libertarian day Marc Hemingway  of The Weekly Standard in hot Air's and  Morrissey asked related question what's  the libertarian position on abortion  Edie specifically asked if libertarian  philosophy puts individual rights  paramount shouldn't libertarians oppose  abortion pretty consistently something  like 30% of self-identified libertarians  are also very strongly pro-life or  anti-abortion I think a lot of people  get to that question in a very similar  way that Ed Morrissey just postulated  it's not a position that I agree with  personally I think abortion should be  legal but it's a position of everything  on the social conservative spectrum that  I have the most respect for  philosophically it is that it's a very  very difficult question a personal  question and I don't think there should  be a litmus test saying you can't be a  libertarian if you don't agree with this  I agree with Matt that there really in  the end there are no litmus test  questions that abortion is not the  deciding question or and your answer to  it is not decisive as to whether or not  you're a libertarian on the thirtieth  anniversary of Roe vs. Wade I wrote a  piece for reason calm talking about how  the kind of social consensus and and to  be blunt the sliding scale of humanity  from fertilization of an egg to the  growth of a fetus to a viable fetus one  that can live outside the outside the  woman outside the mother this is  something that makes intuitive sense to  people it is not a logical answer but  the fact is is that definitions of life  and of death change with time they  change with medical technology and  social understanding what what counted  as a birth in 1900 is not the same as in  2000 or in 1800 I think that we are  getting as we develop more ways of  controlling our sexuality and our  reproduction we are actually seeing the  minimization of abortion as a major  issue in American politics so I respect  the idea of people being against  abortion on demand  I think that they should lobby to change  people's minds but I think that the  legal framework that's in place is  pretty good and that the science  scientific and medical framework that is  in place and is growing will make this  issue less pressing from as the future  unfolds
 

